{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# LLM gate runs record no per-run request or tool-call counts, so the gates cannot be tuned\n\n## The gap\n\n**Nothing durably records how many model requests or tool calls a gate run consumed.** There is no\nusage log on disk, and the audit sidecar carries criteria and citations but no request/tool-call\ncounts. Established while investigating rebar ticket c827: the control-group question\n\"how many of the 240 available requests did the epics that closed cleanly actually use?\" **could not\nbe answered from the store at all.**\n\n## Why this matters — it is why the same bug recurred six times\n\nThe completion gate's step budget has been raised **five times** across its history\n(the history sweep on rebar ticket c827), a hard tool-call cap was proposed and deferred\n(rebar ticket 7c58) then proposed and rejected (rebar ticket b2bb), and the verifier's own creating\nepic was force-closed citing the limitation as \"documented\". **Every one of those decisions was made\nwithout spend data**, because the data was never recorded.\n\nThe cost of that absence is measurable: answering a single question about this gate required **four\nlive instrumented runs at ~20-50 minutes each**, re-deriving numbers that should have been a query.\nAnd the tuning advice the docs give (`the loop-tuning advice in `docs/llm-framework.md`` — \"a high recursion limit means\nyou're paying for a loop, fix the loop\") is unactionable, because nothing tells an operator whether\nthey are paying for a loop or for breadth.\n\nThis is the same **\"computed then discarded\"** shape as two sibling defects: the repetition\ndiagnostic that `interpret_failure` builds and `raise_completion_workflow_failure` drops\n(rebar ticket c827), and the deterministic child-closure proof collapsed to a boolean\n(rebar ticket 6ec8). The value is produced and thrown away one frame from the surface.\n\n## Scope note\n\n`usage_log` machinery already exists in-tree (e.g. `usage_log.failure_usage`,\n`usage_log.capture_attempt_messages`), and `run_ctx.usage` is live inside the tool-filter predicate.\n**Establish what is already captured before adding anything** — the likely defect is that the data is\ncaptured and not persisted, in which case this is a wiring job, not new accounting. Do not build a\nsecond, parallel usage mechanism.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\nEach line is one independently verifiable deliverable.\n\n- [ ] a gate run persists the model requests it consumed\n- [ ] a gate run persists the tool calls it consumed\n- [ ] a gate run persists the applicable `request_limit` and `tool_calls_limit`\n- [ ] a gate run persists the step name, the ticket id, and wall-clock duration\n- [ ] the FAILURE path records all of the above (a run that exhausts its budget or errors is\n      exactly when the numbers are needed, and is the case most likely to be missed)\n- [ ] the SUCCESS path records all of the above, so a clean run is usable as a control group\n- [ ] the distinct-vs-total tool-call-signature counts are recorded, so a LOOP is distinguishable\n      from genuine BREADTH after the fact without re-running\n- [ ] a gate run persists the worst-consecutive-repeat count\n- [ ] a gate run persists `finish_reason` (recorded for triage, deliberately not rendered) (the windowed distinct-ratio that\n      rebar ticket 70bc establishes is computable from the record)\n- [ ] the record is produced by an ordinary gate run with no env var set and no instrumentation\n- [ ] the exact named retrieval command **renders** the tool-call and distinct counts in its own\n      output — proven by pasting that RENDERED output, not a raw JSONL line\n- [ ] the retrieval command's existing token/cost columns are unchanged, pinned by a test\n- [ ] a test pins that the counts are WRITTEN on the success path\n- [ ] a test pins that the counts are WRITTEN on the exhaustion path\n- [ ] the test fixture varies which path is taken (a success-only fixture would be vacuous here)\n- [ ] no second parallel usage mechanism is introduced where the existing one can be wired\n\n## Fix plan — wire the EXISTING mechanism, add nothing parallel\n\nRoot cause (confirmed at runtime; full RCA in the first comment): `usage_log.record()` builds its\nrow from the single allowlist `_FIELDS` (`usage_log.py:32`, iterated at `:311`), so the further\nkeys `failure_usage()` already computes — `tool_calls`, `tool_calls_distinct`,\n`max_consecutive_repeat`, `top_repeated_tool_calls`, `request_limit`, `tool_calls_limit`,\n`finish_reason`, and (once [rebar:70bc-ab05-6d4c-45a0] lands) `distinct_ratio_window` — are\n**computed and then discarded at the write**.\n\n### Step 1 — `record()` stops discarding\n\nAdd a second explicit allowlist `_SHAPE_FIELDS` holding those keys (including\n`distinct_ratio_window`, the loop-vs-breadth discriminator pinned on\n[rebar:c827-2a23-cd00-473e]), written **only when\npresent** in the `usage` dict (absent means \"not measured\"; defaulting to `0` would make a\nshapeless caller assert the falsehood \"this run used no tools\"). Explicit allowlist, not\n`**usage` passthrough, so this module keeps owning its durable schema. Add `duration_s` and\n`ticket` as explicit `record()` keyword arguments.\n\n### Step 2 — the SUCCESS path, and exactly how each field reaches `record()`\n\n`record_call_spend` (`agent_call.py`) receives neither the limits nor the call start time, so the\nplumbing is specified field by field rather than left implicit:\n\n- **`tool_calls`, `tool_calls_distinct`, `max_consecutive_repeat`, `top_repeated_tool_calls`,\n  `distinct_ratio_window`, `request_limit`, `tool_calls_limit`, `finish_reason`** — `runner.py`\n  calls the SAME reducer the\n  failure path uses, `usage_log.run_shape(run_messages, request_limit=req_limit,\n  tool_calls_limit=max(8, eff_max_iter))`. Both limits are *arguments to that call* and the\n  reducer echoes them into its result, which is how they reach the row on the success path. The\n  result is filtered by `usage_log.shape_only()` (which returns exactly `_SHAPE_FIELDS`) and\n  merged into `usage` — deliberately NOT the reducer's token totals, which are message-derived\n  approximations that must never overwrite `_extract_usage`'s authoritative provider figures.\n  `req_limit` and `eff_max_iter` are already in scope from `build_usage_limits`.\n  `run_messages` is already populated on the success path — `capture_attempt_messages` extends\n  the sink from its `finally`, not from an `except` (verified at runtime).\n- **`duration_s`** — computed in `runner.py` as `time.monotonic() - _t0` (`_t0` is the existing\n  call-start marker) and passed as a new optional kwarg through `record_call_spend` to `record`.\n- **`ticket`** — `req.target.get(\"ticket_id\")`, passed the same way.\n- The failure path passes `duration_s`/`ticket` through `record_failure` as keyword-only\n  arguments with defaults, so its existing positional signature is unbroken.\n- BOTH paths are wrapped in a swallow-and-log guard, not just the success one: telemetry must\n  never break the call it measures. On the success path `runner.py` wraps the shape capture;\n  on the FAILURE path `record_failure` already wraps its ENTIRE body — the shape capture, the\n  `duration_s`/`ticket` forwarding and the `record()` write alike — and that is load-bearing\n  there, because its caller is an `except` block whose one job is to re-raise the provider's\n  exception unchanged: an error escaping telemetry would REPLACE that exception and destroy\n  the real diagnosis.\n\n### Step 3 — gate runs record with no env var set\n\n`record()` resolves its sink as `REBAR_USAGE_LOG` (explicit, still wins) -> else, **iff\n`in_gate_session()`**, the repo-local `<repo root>/.rebar/usage.jsonl` when that directory already\nexists -> else no-op exactly as today. `in_gate_session()` is the existing fail-closed marker\nevery code-reading gate binds through the single chokepoint `gate_source.gate_read_root`\n(`gate_source.py:110`, entered by `plan_review/__init__.py:459` and `completion.py:485`), so this\nneeds no new plumbing. `.rebar/*` is already gitignored and already documented as the home for\nlocal rebar state. Every non-gate caller stays byte-identical, preserving the module's\nno-op-when-unset guarantee for the callers it was written for.\n\n### Step 4 — the retrieval command must RENDER the new fields\n\n`summarize()` today folds only the token `_FIELDS`, so a row can carry `tool_calls=125,\ntool_calls_distinct=1` and the operator running the named command still sees only token counters.\n**A fix that leaves the signal written but not displayed reproduces the very \"computed then\ndiscarded\" pattern this ticket exists to remove, one layer further out.** So `summarize()` gains a\nper-op run-shape section rendering total vs distinct tool calls and the distinct ratio, plus the\nlimits and the worst consecutive-repeat run, so an operator reading one command's output can tell\na LOOP from BREADTH.\n\nConstraint: the existing token/cost table is **unchanged** — other callers depend on its shape\n(`$GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY` on the two billable weekly jobs, and\n`tests/unit/test_usage_log*.py` assert on it). The new section is additive, and rows carrying no\nshape fields (every pre-aec1 row) must still summarize without error. A test pins the existing\ntoken table AND the new section.\n\n## Retrieval\n\n    python -m rebar.llm.usage_log summarize .rebar/usage.jsonl\n\n## Testing\n\n    pytest -m \"not integration and not external\" -n 4 --dist worksteal -q\n    make lint && make typecheck\n\n`tests/unit/test_usage_log_run_shape_aec1.py` drives the REAL runner offline (`FunctionModel` +\n`ALLOW_MODEL_REQUESTS=False`, the established pattern in `test_usage_log_failed_calls.py`) and\nreads the written FILE, varying the path axis three ways: `_ok_model` (success),\n`_breadth_model` (all-distinct exploration, terminates), `_looping_model` (one repeated\nsignature, runs to budget exhaustion).\n\n- the exhaustion-path test asserts `tool_calls`, `request_limit`, `tool_calls_limit` are written\n- the success-path test asserts `tool_calls` and `tool_calls_distinct` are written, **and\n  explicitly asserts `request_limit`, `tool_calls_limit` and `duration_s` are present**, so the\n  Step-2 plumbing cannot silently regress to failure-path-only\n- one test asserts the recorded distinct-vs-total ratio separates a loop from breadth FROM THE\n  RECORD ALONE\n- a control test asserts a NON-gate run still writes nothing when the env var is unset, so the\n  gate-sink test cannot pass for the trivial reason that everything writes\n- a rendering test asserts `summarize()` output CONTAINS the tool-call and distinct counts, and a\n  second asserts its existing token/cost table is byte-unchanged"}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785956152658374001,"uuid":"bd8f5a53-e0ea-4f53-a942-1aae97183da3"}